    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: Ethics, Media & Social Responsibility (S5-U1)\nCOURSE: 8177 Sports Entertainment and Event Management\nUNIT OBJECTIVE: Students can identify ethical issues, investigate social/environmental concerns, examine media bias effects on public perception, and describe community value of the industry.\nPLANNED WORK PRODUCT: Three deliverables: (1) Social Media & Communications Policy — one page, approved by General Manager. (2) Esports Code of Conduct — drafted by League Manager with input from both classes. (3) Ethical case analysis — each student analyzes one real ethical incident from the sports, entertainment, or esports industry and presents findings: what happened, what the organization did, what they should have done, and what the conglomerate learns from it.\nCOURSE ROLE: General Manager approves the Social Media Policy and Esports Code of Conduct. Events Manager reviews for event-specific implications (what can be posted during a live event, how complaints at events are handled). All 8177 students complete the media bias analysis using a provided pair of articles covering the same esports or sports event from different perspectives.\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8177-63, 8177-64, 8177-65, 8177-66\n\nCRITERIA\n\nCriterion 1: Exploring Ethical Issues in the Sports, Entertainment, and Event Industries\n- 8177-63 Identify ethical issues related to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * amateur, collegiate, and professional athletics\n    * entertainment and event industries\n    * sports and entertainment media\n    * social media\n- 8177-64 Investigate how social and environmental concerns affect decisions in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * community-driven projects\n    * relationships with charitable partners\n    * sustainable practices and procedures\n    * corporate social responsibility guidelines\n- 8177-65 Examine the effect of media bias on public perception and opinion.\n    * Examination should include the dangers of persuasive and sensational tactics, and how these influence public relations in sports, entertainment, and event management.\n- 8177-66 Describe the value of the sports, entertainment, and event industries to the community.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * encouraging a sense of community\n    * advocating for health and wellness awareness\n    * elevating community well-being\n    * encouraging self-improvement through meaningful experiences\n    * supporting economic and business development\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work passes when the planned work product does what the assignment asks and shows each competency listed above in at least one specific way, in the student's own words, applied to the class conglomerate (its divisions, school store, events and esports program) or a real organization in sports, entertainment or events, and uses the unit vocabulary accurately (Business ethics, Reputational risk, Media bias, Social responsibility, Escalation path, Code of conduct, Transparency). Specific examples, not general statements. Work that restates definitions without applying them, or leaves a listed competency with no evidence at all, does not pass. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required.",
    "promptText": "Ethics, Media & Social Responsibility (S5-U1)\n\nStudents can identify ethical issues, investigate social/environmental concerns, examine media bias effects on public perception, and describe community value of the industry.\n\nWhat you will make: Three deliverables: (1) Social Media & Communications Policy — one page, approved by General Manager. (2) Esports Code of Conduct — drafted by League Manager with input from both classes. (3) Ethical case analysis — each student analyzes one real ethical incident from the sports, entertainment, or esports industry and presents findings: what happened, what the organization did, what they should have done, and what the conglomerate learns from it.\n\nYour role: General Manager approves the Social Media Policy and Esports Code of Conduct. Events Manager reviews for event-specific implications (what can be posted during a live event, how complaints at events are handled). All 8177 students complete the media bias analysis using a provided pair of articles covering the same esports or sports event from different perspectives.\n\nThe work: Students draft the conglomerate's Social Media & Communications Policy — a one-page document that establishes what can be posted, who approves it, how negative feedback is handled, and what the escalation path is for a reputational incident. This becomes a standing operating document for the Media & PR division.\n\nGo deeper:\n\n- Media bias examination (Task 65): students compare two pieces of coverage of the same real sports or esports event from sources with different editorial perspectives. They identify specific language choices, framing decisions, and omissions that reveal bias. How does this inform how the conglomerate produces its own media?\n- Social and environmental impact (Task 64): students audit the conglomerate's environmental footprint — sublimation inks, heat press energy use, packaging, event waste. They propose one realistic operational change that reduces impact without eliminating the production capability.\n- Community value articulation (Task 66): management students write a one-page community value statement for the conglomerate — what does this program contribute to the school, and how would the school community be different without it? This document is referenced in the administrator reporting artifact.\n\nYour work must show evidence of each of these:\n\n- Identify ethical issues related to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n- Investigate how social and environmental concerns affect decisions in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n- Examine the effect of media bias on public perception and opinion.\n- Describe the value of the sports, entertainment, and event industries to the community.\n\nUse these terms accurately: Business ethics, Reputational risk, Media bias, Social responsibility, Escalation path, Code of conduct, Transparency.\n\nWrite your response under “Your response” below."
